  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe are to find a step function h on [0,2] with binary values and exactly 303 positions set to 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap with a shifted version of itself. This is an instance of a discrete optimization problem known to be NP-hard. The current record C5 value is 0.380880, and we aim to achieve a value below 0.3808. Our approach employs a tailored implementation of the Blossom algorithm, which solves the minimum cost perfect matching problem in a general graph with arbitrary edge weights. This method is particularly suited because we can transform the overlap minimization problem into solving a minimum weighted matching problem on a triangular hypergraph.\n\nThe central idea is to model the optimization as a minimum cost perfect matching problem. We represent each shift distance as a potential edge between positions in the grid, and the cost of an edge is the overlap between the 1's of chosen function pairs. Our work leverages the Blossom algorithm's ability to handle large-scale graphs efficiently due to its use of successive convexifications and fast heuristics for weight reductions.\n\nWe address the non-differentiability and combinatorial nature of the problem by using minimum cost perfect matching, a well-proven mathematical programming technique. The main limitation is the O(n^3) complexity, which we mitigate by using state-of-the-art algorithms from the Blossom codebase. This approach is expected to deliver significant improvements over the parent solution's method.\n\nMathematical specification\nProblem representation:\n- Input: Grid points x_i = i * (2/606) for i=0 to 605\n- Binary variables h_i \u2208 {0,1} for each grid point\n- Constant: Total sum of h_i must be 303\n\nObjective function:\nMinimize over all bijections \u03c0 between vertices in {0,...,605}:\n  C5 = max_{k} (1/dx) * \u222boperation=h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nConverted into a minimum cost perfect matching problem:\n- Define a triangular hypergraph with edges for all distance-\u03c4 shifts\n- Edge cost matrix: C[i,j] = I{i != j} * [overlap between h_i and h_j]\n- Requires matching each node to itself and \u03c4 neighbors for all \u03c4\n\nConstraints:\n- h_i \u2208 {0,1} for all i\n- \u2211h_i = 303\n- Matching must be perfect\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialize parent solution if available\n2. Construct distance graph with \u03c4 ranging from -303 to 303\n3. Formulate as minimum cost perfect matching with edge costs being overlap values\n4. Use Blossom algorithm:\n   - Initialize with a matching of diagonal edges (\u03c4=0)\n   - Successive weight reductions using heuristic methods\n   - Relax weights via soft-clustering\n   - Fast rerooting for consecutive moves\n5. Track the best solution found by reporting the lowest C5\n\nSearch choices and variations\n- Start with \u03c4 = 0, expand range incrementally\n- Use blossom_mult_cost.py implementation\n- Allow parallelization over three CPUs\n- Enable progressive weight reductions\n  - Initial weight reduction factor: 1.5\n  - Step factor reduction: 0.8 per major loop\n- Include fallback to recursive blossoms if heuristic fails\n- Use \u03c4-vectorization to minimize correlation computations\n\nComputational budget\n- Allocate 700 seconds to Blossom algorithm loops\n- Reserve 400 seconds for continuous-to-binary projection\n- 200 seconds for deep pruning of high-cost edges\n- 200 seconds for residual optimization\n- Total clock time: 1300 seconds\n- Can handle 606-node graph with progressive reductions\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Validate: All h[i] \u2208 {0,1}, \u2211h_i = 303\n- Compute C5 via np.correlate(, mode='full')*dx\n- Ensure finite value (C5), dimension n=606\n- Return h_values, C5_bound, n_points=606\n- Fallback: Return parent solution if timer expires\n- Optimization: Enabled if time permits\n- FFD schedule: 3 parallel blossom cores @ 400s each\n- Hash filtering to remove equivalent moves\n- Progressive pruning of \u03c4-values based on initial run\n\nEdge cases: Handle singular detection automatically (\u03c4=0.5), use logarithmic weighting for zero-cost edges.",
